{
  "background_and_question": {
    "claims": [
      {
        "attribution": "Richards",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "d9bcfe43d5d8593b765f8ba8dacca1517d1601e340f4c84c2eb9cfe519861c48",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-d9bcfe43d5d8-0000-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 2,
              "start": 2
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Richards, inmate Kyle Richards alleges that prison employee Thomas Perttu sexually harassed him and other inmates, destroyed grievance documents, and retaliated against him for attempting to file them."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Court",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "d9bcfe43d5d8593b765f8ba8dacca1517d1601e340f4c84c2eb9cfe519861c48",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-d9bcfe43d5d8-0000-0007"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 2,
              "start": 2
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The Magistrate Judge found a genuine issue of fact as to whether the plaintiffs were excused from exhausting their claims because of Perttu\u2019s interference, and held that the issue was appropriate for resolution during an evidentiary hearing."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Court",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "d9bcfe43d5d8593b765f8ba8dacca1517d1601e340f4c84c2eb9cfe519861c48",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-d9bcfe43d5d8-0000-0008"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The usual practice is that factual disputes regarding legal claims go to a jury, even if a judge could ordinarily resolve the issues himself."
      }
    ],
    "heading": "Background and Question",
    "status": "complete",
    "summary": null,
    "summary_citations": []
  },
  "case_id": "scotus-23-1324",
  "decision": {
    "claims": [
      {
        "attribution": "Court",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "d9bcfe43d5d8593b765f8ba8dacca1517d1601e340f4c84c2eb9cfe519861c48",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-d9bcfe43d5d8-0001-0002"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 14,
              "start": 14
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The Court held that nothing in the PLRA prevents a jury trial on exhaustion disputes."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Court",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "d9bcfe43d5d8593b765f8ba8dacca1517d1601e340f4c84c2eb9cfe519861c48",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-d9bcfe43d5d8-0001-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 13,
              "start": 13
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "District courts must structure their order of operations to preserve the right to a jury trial when exhaustion issues are intertwined with other claims."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Court",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "d9bcfe43d5d8593b765f8ba8dacca1517d1601e340f4c84c2eb9cfe519861c48",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-d9bcfe43d5d8-0001-0004"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 18,
              "start": 18
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The principle of Beacon Theatres applies when judicial resolution of a common issue might prevent a full jury trial for reasons other than estoppel."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Court",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "d9bcfe43d5d8593b765f8ba8dacca1517d1601e340f4c84c2eb9cfe519861c48",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-d9bcfe43d5d8-0002-0003"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 21,
              "start": 21
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The Court affirmed the judgment of the United States Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "d9bcfe43d5d8593b765f8ba8dacca1517d1601e340f4c84c2eb9cfe519861c48",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-d9bcfe43d5d8-0002-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 21,
              "start": 21
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Justice Barrett, in dissent, noted that if Congress had required judges to resolve exhaustion disputes, the Court would need to address a potential Seventh Amendment violation, but the Court chose not to raise that constitutional question."
      }
    ],
